- description: Queries ABOM runs against ABOM's own workflows

# Narrower than abom-findings.qls on purpose. This suite is a gate on one repository at review
# time, not the attribution corpus: a finding here has to be worth blocking a pull request
# for, so the experimental queries that abom-findings.qls keeps are absent.
- import: codeql-suites/actions-security-and-quality.qls
  from: codeql/actions-queries
  version: 0.6.35

# Retains `Debug/SyntaxError` and `Diagnostics/SuccessfullyExtractedFiles`, which the upstream
# suite includes and which report nothing about security. They stay because a self audit that
# cannot distinguish "no findings" from "nothing was extracted" is worse than no self audit.

# `actions/unpinned-tag` stays in. Every `uses:` in this repository carries an inline full-length
# commit SHA, so the rule has nothing to report here and remains the check that says so.
#
# `ActionsLockAware/UncoveredActionRef.ql` is deliberately *not* in this suite, though it is in
# `abom-findings.qls`. It compares a workflow's ref against `.github/workflows/actions.lock`, and
# this repository's lock keys its entries on the tags those inline SHAs were resolved from, so the
# query would report every workflow ref. That is a true statement about the lock's adoption state
# and a false one about whether this repository is pinned, and only `gh actions-lock` can resolve
# it -- the lock is generated, and hand-editing it would make it name commits nobody resolved. The
# self audit reports that gap without gating on it; see `.github/workflows/self-audit.yml`.

# What the lockfile's absence would mean here. This repository keeps a lock, so this is silent --
# and it fires if that ever stops being true, including if a toolchain change stops the lockfile
# reaching the database, which would otherwise read as a clean gate.
- query: ActionsLockAware/ActionsLockMissingForWorkflow.ql
  from: advanced-security/abom-actions-findings-src
